---
layout: base.njk
pageType: home
title: proofstone — engineering roadmaps where every milestone is a proof
description: Engineering roadmaps where every milestone is a proof — a verifiable artifact, not a keyword. Built for working engineers.
---
{#- Static and short. The three scroll-driven scenes that used to open this page
    are gone — with them the road, the stones, the keyword cloud, the typing
    artifact strip and the "Skip visuals" escape hatch that only existed because
    there was something to escape. What is left is the shape of the argument:
    thesis, the maps themselves, then how a milestone works.
    The counts stay computed — they are the one number on this page and
    hardcoding them is how this project burned twice. #}
<section class="hero">
  <p class="hero__kicker">Engineering roadmaps &#183; {{ liveRoadmaps.length }} live &#183; {{ reviewRoadmaps.length }} in review</p>
  <h1 class="hero__title">{{ site.tagline }}</h1>
  <p class="hero__sub">{{ site.thesisSub }}</p>
  <p class="hero__series">{{ site.seriesLine }}</p>
</section>

{#- "Saw it → clicked it → reading it": nothing stands between the visitor and a
    roadmap. The showcase is the second thing on the page, and the explanation of
    the format comes after it rather than before. #}
<section class="roadmaps" id="roadmaps" aria-label="Roadmaps">
  {#- The category is said once, in words, above the group it applies to — and
      the count is computed here like every other number on this page. #}
  {%- if liveRoadmaps.length %}
  <p class="shelf-cap"><span class="shelf-cap__text">Live maps</span><span class="shelf-cap__n">{{ liveRoadmaps.length }}</span></p>
  {%- endif %}
  {%- for r in liveRoadmaps %}
  <article class="card">
    <a class="card__main" href="/{{ r.slug }}/">
      {#- The face of the card is the roadmap's own shape — spine, fork, column
          with branches — derived from the SAME assets/roadmap.svg that the
          roadmap page inlines. One source of geometry, so a card can never show
          a shape the map does not have. The filter strips the labels, the status
          plates, the legend and the <defs> marker (id="arw" exists in all three
          maps, and three copies on one page is a triple duplicate id).
          aria-hidden lives inside the derived SVG: this is a preview standing in
          for a link, not a figure, and the link is already named by the title. #}
      <div class="card__map">{{ r | roadmapSilhouette | safe }}</div>
      <div class="card__body">
        {#- `order` is the registry's own field, printed with a leading zero — it
            is a shelf mark, not a ranking. In a grid it is a mark again: the eye
            scans the silhouettes, so the number does not need to be a monument. #}
        <p class="card__head">
          <span class="card__num"><span class="card__num-pre">No.</span><span class="card__num-fig">{% if r.order < 10 %}0{% endif %}{{ r.order }}</span></span>
          <span class="stamp">Live</span>
        </p>
        <h2 class="card__title">{{ r.title }}</h2>
        <p class="card__tagline">{{ r.cardLine }}</p>
        <p class="card__meta">
          <span class="badge badge--count">{{ r.milestones }} milestones</span>
          <span class="badge badge--flagship">{{ r | roadmapStars }} flagship</span>
          <span class="card__go"><svg class="arrow" viewBox="0 0 20 10" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M0 5h17M13 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></span>
        </p>
      </div>
    </a>
    {%- if r.lab %}
    <p class="card__lab">
      <span class="card__lab-tag">lab</span>
      <a href="{{ r.lab.url }}" rel="noopener"><code>{{ r.lab.name }}</code></a> — {{ r.lab.note }}.
    </p>
    {%- endif %}
  </article>
  {%- endfor %}

  {%- if reviewRoadmaps.length %}
  <p class="shelf-cap"><span class="shelf-cap__text">In practitioner review</span><span class="shelf-cap__n">{{ reviewRoadmaps.length }}</span></p>
  {%- endif %}
  {#- No silhouette on a review card, and no placeholder either: these maps are
      not published yet, and a framed box saying "map coming later" is a
      placeholder occupying space for its own sake. The card is text. #}
  {%- for r in reviewRoadmaps %}
  <article class="card card--review">
    <div class="card__body">
      <p class="card__head">
        <span class="card__num"><span class="card__num-pre">No.</span><span class="card__num-fig">{% if r.order < 10 %}0{% endif %}{{ r.order }}</span></span>
        <span class="stamp stamp--open">In review</span>
      </p>
      <h2 class="card__title">{{ r.title }}</h2>
      <p class="card__tagline">{{ r.cardLine }}</p>
      <p class="card__meta">
        <span class="badge badge--count">{{ r.milestones }} milestones</span>
        <span class="badge badge--flagship">{{ r | roadmapStars }} flagship</span>
        <span class="badge badge--review">private until published</span>
      </p>
    </div>
    {%- if r.labNote %}
    <p class="card__lab"><span class="card__lab-tag">lab</span> {{ r.labNote }}.</p>
    {%- endif %}
  </article>
  {%- endfor %}
</section>

<section class="how">
  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <ol class="how__steps">
    <li><span class="how__num">1</span><span class="how__body"><strong>You're done when …</strong> Each node states one finishing condition, in advance.</span></li>
    <li><span class="how__num">2</span><span class="how__body"><strong>An artifact.</strong> Meeting it leaves something outside your head — a merged eval, a green test suite, a fabricated board.</span></li>
    <li><span class="how__num">3</span><span class="how__body"><strong>Proof.</strong> The artifact is the evidence. Nothing here is finished by reading about it.</span></li>
  </ol>
</section>

{#- One real milestone, rendered from a real README. This is the only place on the
    home page where a visitor sees an actual PROOF stamp and an actual "You're
    done when …" rather than a sentence describing one — which is why it survived
    the scenes it used to live inside. Static: no timeline, no script, no reveal.
    The milestone is named in the registry (sampleMilestone), so which one is
    shown is a registry decision and the build fails if it stops resolving. #}
<section class="sample" aria-labelledby="sample-heading">
  <h2 class="sample__title" id="sample-heading">What one milestone looks like</h2>
  <p class="sample__lead">Taken from a live map, as it is rendered there.</p>
  <div class="prose sample__block">
    {%- for r in liveRoadmaps %}{% if r.sampleMilestone %}{{ r | milestoneSample(r.sampleMilestone) | safe }}{% endif %}{% endfor %}
  </div>
  <p class="sample__verdict"><span class="stamp stamp--ok">Proven</span> That is the whole format.</p>
</section>

{#- Related work sits here for the same reason it sits near the end of every
    roadmap README: it is a footnote to the argument, not part of it. The reader
    has just been told what a milestone is, which is what makes "this one has no
    milestones" mean something. #}
<section class="companions" aria-labelledby="companions-heading">
  <h2 class="companions__heading" id="companions-heading">Companion projects</h2>
  <p class="companions__lead">
    Standalone repositories alongside the series. Not roadmaps themselves — no milestones,
    no finishing conditions — but the maps link into them from the inside.
  </p>

  {#- A block-paragraph, not a grid: there is one companion, and a one-cell
      catalogue reads as a row that failed to load. A second one appends another
      .companion here and nothing else changes.
      The owner is spelled out in the link text on purpose — this repository
      lives in a personal account, not in the proofstone org, and the page must
      not imply otherwise. #}
  <div class="companion">
    <h3 class="companion__name">
      <span class="companion__tag">index</span>
      <a href="https://github.com/TheSeydiCharyyev/build-your-own-agent" rel="noopener"><code>TheSeydiCharyyev/build-your-own-agent</code></a>
    </h3>
    <p class="companion__note">
      An index, not a map. For each of the ten components of the agent stack it points at the
      best from-scratch tutorials that exist elsewhere; for the three where none did — MCP
      server and client, coding agent, token accounting — it ships a runnable reference
      implementation of its own, zero dependencies and no API key. The AI Safety map links
      into it twice and states the relation there: that roadmap proves an agent is safe,
      this repository is the half where you make one work.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="thesis">
  <h2>Why proof, not keywords</h2>
  <p>{{ site.thesis }}</p>
  <p class="thesis__contribute">
    The roadmap repositories are the source of truth — this site is the render layer.
    Contribute by opening a pull request on a roadmap's repo; the site rebuilds itself.
    <a href="{{ site.org }}" rel="noopener">github.com/proofstone <svg class="arrow" viewBox="0 0 20 10" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M0 5h17M13 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a>
  </p>
</section>
